+## Objective
+
+Fix the following confirmed defect: repair the named cause, nothing else. This increment is past the owner's three-round review ceiling. This one item is in the blocking classes, data integrity (a mutant recorded killed that no test caught), and Review A of round 13 reproduced it. Every finding recorded in `docs/HARDENING.md` under TASK-031 stays there and is not yours to fix.
+
+**Root cause, in one sentence.** Round 13's `remove_bytecode_from_copy` in `bridge/cursor-bridge/mutation-run.py` deletes every file ending in `.pyc` anywhere in the copy, not only folders named `__pycache__`, so a project that commits `.pyc` files as test data (a decompiler, a disassembler, a packaging tool) has those tests fail in the copy for a reason that is not the mutant, and an equivalent mutant is recorded killed, exit 0 (a false kill).
+
+**Evidence.**
+- Review A of round 13 reproduced it: a tracked `tests/data/sample.pyc` that a test reads is absent from the copy, the test fails with the file missing, and an equivalent mutant is recorded `killed (ran)`, exit 0.
+- The defect is in round 13's brief (its Requirement 2 named "or `.pyc` file"), not in the builder's reading of it.
+- A loose `.pyc` beside no source is never read for a module: the import system prefers the source suffixes to `.pyc` in the same folder, and stale bytecode for a source module is read only from `__pycache__`. So the loose-file branch is not needed to keep round 13's fix.
